+## Naming: `site` vs `bounded.tools`
+
+Two different repos share the "bounded.tools" name, and it is easy to land in
+the wrong one:
+
+- **This repo is [`bounded-systems/site`][site-repo]** — the static website
+  served at <https://bounded.tools>. Its identifier everywhere it matters is
+  `bounded-tools-site` (`package.json` name, the Worker name in
+  `wrangler.jsonc`, the flake `pname`, the GHCR image
+  `ghcr.io/bounded-systems/bounded-tools-site`). The title above is the
+  *product* (the domain), not the repo.
+- **[`bounded-systems/bounded.tools`][hooks-repo] is not the website.** It is
+  a separate, unrelated project: the GitHub App receiver for `prx`, a
+  TypeScript Cloudflare Worker deployed at `hooks.bounded.tools`. It does not
+  build, contain, or deploy this site.
+
+The trap: an earlier version of this README told people to clone
+`bounded.tools.git`, so a local checkout of *this* repo often lives in a
+directory literally named `…/bounded-systems/bounded.tools/` while its
+`origin` points at `site.git`. Anything that keys on the directory name — a
+human, a script, an agent — then resolves to the wrong GitHub repo.
+
+- The directory name is never the source of truth; `git remote get-url origin`
+  is.
+- The canonical local path is `…/bounded-systems/site/`. If you have a stale
+  checkout, `mv bounded.tools site` (or re-clone per **Setup** below) — nothing
+  in the build depends on the directory name.
+
+**Proposed rename (not executed):** neither GitHub repo should be renamed to
+fix this. `site` is what everything deploy-side is wired to under the
+`bounded-tools-site` name, and `bounded.tools` is a live Worker on
+`hooks.bounded.tools`. Renaming either buys little and risks a deploy. If a
+maintainer later wants the collision gone at the GitHub level, the
+least-disruptive option is renaming `bounded-systems/bounded.tools` to
+something that says what it is (e.g. `prx-receiver`; GitHub keeps redirects
+for renamed repos), *not* renaming `site`. Until then, the two READMEs
+cross-link and local clones are named `site/`. Tracked in [#32][issue-32].
+
 ## How it consumes the brand
 
 `brand/` is a **git submodule** pinned to a commit of [`bounded-systems/brand`][brand].
